(window.LEARN_C = window.LEARN_C || {})["chem-11-01"] = {"1.1":{"s":"Chemistry is the study of matter: what it is made of, how it behaves, and how it changes. Everything around us, from food and medicines to fuels and fabrics, is made of chemical substances. This chapter starts the course with the basic ideas that all later chapters use: the nature and properties of matter, the laws of chemical combination, atoms and molecules, and the mole."},"1.2":{"s":"Matter is anything that has mass and occupies space. It exists in three physical states, solid, liquid and gas. Pure substances are elements and compounds. An element has one kind of atom, and a compound has atoms of different elements joined in a fixed ratio. Mixtures are formed by physically mixing substances. A homogeneous mixture has the same composition throughout; a heterogeneous mixture does not.","w":["A compound has a fixed composition and different properties from its elements; a mixture has a variable composition."]},"1.3":{"s":"The properties of matter are physical or chemical. Physical properties, such as colour, melting point, boiling point and density, can be measured or seen without changing what the substance is. Chemical properties show up only when the substance reacts and changes into something else. Measurements are written with a number and a unit, and the SI system is the standard set of units used all over the world.","w":["Burning, rusting and souring are chemical changes; melting and boiling are physical changes.","A measurement is incomplete without its unit."]},"1.4":{"s":"Chemical combination follows a few laws. The law of conservation of mass: matter is neither created nor destroyed in a reaction. The law of definite proportions: a pure compound always has the same elements in the same ratio by mass. The law of multiple proportions: when two elements form more than one compound, the masses of one that combine with a fixed mass of the other are in a simple whole-number ratio. Gay-Lussac's law deals with gas volumes.","w":["Match each law to its example: water always has 1 part hydrogen to 8 parts oxygen by mass (definite proportions); CO and CO₂ (multiple proportions).","Mass is conserved in a reaction, but the number of molecules need not be."]},"1.5":{"s":"Avogadro's law: two gas samples of the same volume, measured at one temperature and one pressure, contain the same number of molecules, whatever the gas. It explains Gay-Lussac's observation that gases react in simple volume ratios. The law also led to the idea that gas molecules such as hydrogen and oxygen are made of two atoms. It is the base of the molar volume of a gas.","w":["Avogadro's law needs the same temperature and pressure; it is about the number of molecules, not the mass."]},"1.6":{"s":"Dalton's atomic theory, put forward in 1808, says that matter is made of indivisible atoms. All atoms of an element are alike, and they differ from the atoms of other elements. Atoms combine in small whole-number ratios to make compounds, and a reaction only rearranges atoms; they are neither created nor destroyed. The theory explains the laws of chemical combination. Later work showed that atoms are divisible and that isotopes exist.","w":["Parts of Dalton's theory were later changed: atoms are divisible, and atoms of one element can have different masses (isotopes)."]},"1.7":{"s":"The mass of an atom is tiny, so it is given in atomic mass units. One atomic mass unit, u, is one twelfth of the mass of a carbon-12 atom. The atomic mass of an element is its mass in u, averaged over its natural isotopes. The molecular mass of a molecule is the sum of the atomic masses of the atoms in it. For ionic compounds the term formula mass is used.","w":["Atomic mass is an average over the isotopes, so it is usually not a whole number."]},"1.8":{"s":"A mole is the amount of a substance that has as many particles as there are atoms in 12 g of carbon-12. This number is Avogadro's constant, about 6.022 × 10²³ per mole. The molar mass of a substance is the mass of one mole in grams; it has the same number as the atomic or molecular mass in u. These two facts link the mass of a sample to the number of moles and to the number of particles.","f":[{"x":"N_A = 6.022*10^23 mol^-1","n":"Avogadro's constant: number of particles in one mole.","r":"Balbharati Chemistry XI, 1.8, p.9"},{"x":"n = mass/molar mass","n":"Number of moles n; the mass in grams and the molar mass in g/mol.","r":"Balbharati Chemistry XI, 1.8, p.9"},{"x":"N = n*N_A","n":"Number of particles N (atoms, molecules or ions) in n moles.","r":"Balbharati Chemistry XI, 1.8, p.9"}],"w":["Molar mass is in g/mol, atomic or molecular mass is in u: the numbers are equal but the units differ.","Mole is a count of particles: say whether you mean moles of atoms or moles of molecules (1 mol O₂ has 2 mol O atoms)."]},"1.9":{"s":"Gases are easier to measure by volume than by mass. By Avogadro's law, one mole of any gas takes the same volume under the same conditions. At standard temperature and pressure, this molar volume is 22.4 dm³ per mole. So the number of moles of a gas equals its volume at STP divided by the molar volume. Multiplying by Avogadro's constant then gives the number of molecules.","f":[{"x":"n = V/22.4 dm^3 mol^-1","n":"Moles of a gas from its volume V at STP (0 degree Celsius, 1 atm).","r":"Balbharati Chemistry XI, 1.9, p.10"}],"w":["22.4 dm³ per mole is for the old STP (0 °C and 1 atm). With 1 bar the molar volume is 22.71 L.","Molar volume applies to gases only, not to liquids or solids."]}};
